WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.155
Keeping test cases out of your prompts.

2
00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:06.787
The quietest way to ruin a test set is to let it leak into the thing it tests.

3
00:00:07.067 --> 00:00:16.923
If a graded case, or its answer, appears in the system prompt or as a few-shot example, the score partly measures whether the model can repeat what it was just shown.

4
00:00:17.203 --> 00:00:28.637
It is the same problem as benchmark contamination, which Deng and colleagues (2023) found evidence of in public benchmarks, except that here you caused it yourself.

5
00:00:28.917 --> 00:00:30.347
Leaks happen innocently.

6
00:00:30.627 --> 00:00:35.213
Someone fixing a failure pastes the failing case into the prompt as an example.

7
00:00:35.493 --> 00:00:40.229
A prompt-optimization loop is tuned against the test set until it memorizes it.

8
00:00:40.509 --> 00:00:46.739
A judge prompt (next week) is given a few labeled examples from the same pool it is later scored on.

9
00:00:47.019 --> 00:00:50.539
The fix is the discipline from machine learning: separate pools.

10
00:00:50.819 --> 00:01:04.536
The evals FAQ describes it for LLM judges: a small training split whose examples may go into the judge’s prompt, a development split you iterate against, and a test split you leave untouched as a final check.

11
00:01:04.816 --> 00:01:11.024
Repeatedly tuning against the development split slowly overfits to it, which is why the test split exists.

12
00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:19.368
Apply the same rule to your system prompt: few-shot examples come from a development pool, never from the golden or regression sets.

13
00:01:19.648 --> 00:01:22.976
Then check mechanically, because good intentions don’t scale.

14
00:01:23.256 --> 00:01:28.461
The exercise below flags any test input that shares a long run of words with the prompt.
